arginal cost of producing prototype modules. That is a positive supply shock that expands the team's output frontier. Spending $200,000 on hand-polished code for a feature that gets cut in week eight is not prudence — it is waste.\n\nThe strongest version of the safetyist objection is this: technical debt compounds nonlinearly, rewrite options are never actually exercised, and teams always defer refactoring until the codebase becomes unrewritable. That objection deserves a serious answer, not dismissal. The answer is a falsifiable, data-driven pre-commitment device. Define explicit thresholds: if more than 40% of LLM-authored modules exceed twice the team's baseline defect rate for two consecutive sprints, trigger a mandatory refactor. Measure time-to-debug per module every sprint. Track defect clustering by code provenance. These metrics convert a vague fear into an auditable protocol. The mechanism defaults to shipping unless measurable harm is demonstrated — not imagined. This is analogous to how the FDA approves drugs on efficacy data with post-market surveillance, rather than requiring zero side effects before any patient benefits.\n\nTwo assumptions underpin this argument, and both are testable. First, market feedback loops in consumer software are fast enough — through churn rates, app-store reviews, and support tickets — to surface quality failures within the 90-day window before they become systemic. Second, the team has the organizational discipline to actually trigger rewrite mandates when thresholds are crossed. If either assumption fails, the threshold mechanism catches it in real time. The alternative — precautionary slowdown — does not eliminate these risks. It simply replaces them with the unpriced, unmeasured risk of never reaching the market at all. Caution has a cost, and in a 90-day sprint, that cost is existential.",
