view cognitively harder per line, not easier. Reviewers cannot apply consistent quality assumptions. They must treat every AI-authored module as a black box that might be correct or might detonate in production. Any honest productivity claim must include total cycle time — generation plus verification — or it is measuring the speedometer while ignoring the brakes.\n\nThe Accelerationist's proposed threshold mechanism — trigger a refactor when defect rates hit 40% — is well-designed on paper. I want to steelman it fully: it is falsifiable, it is measurable, and it creates an explicit decision point. That is more intellectual honesty than most governance proposals offer. But it assumes the very organizational discipline that most teams lack. The proof-of-concept trap is well-documented: organizations fund pilots, celebrate demos, and then never invest in scaling or refactoring. The executive-engineering communication gap means that when the board meeting is tomorrow and the defect rate is at 38%, no one pulls the trigger. My falsifiability test for the Accelerationist: what is their prediction about how often organizations will actually enforce the threshold under shipping pressure? If the answer is \"most of the time,\" I would point them to the exposed base rate. Gartner estimated in 2023 that 85% of AI projects fail to move beyond pilot stage. The rewrite option is never exercised because the incentive structure always favors the next feature over paying down debt.\n\nTwo assumptions underpin my argument, and I will name them. First, I assume AI code quality is jagged and unpredictable rather than uniformly mediocre. If someone demonstrates consistent, predictable LLM output quality across the full range of sprint tasks — not just benchmarks — this argument weakens. Current evidence strongly supports jaggedness. Second, I assume engineering teams in growth-stage companies lack sufficient organizational power to refuse debt-laden sprints. If engineers have genuine veto power over code quality, the cost-externalization argument softens. But the documented norm is executive override, not engineering autonomy. I am not arguing for paralysis. AI-generated boilerplate, scaffolding, and test stubs can save real time where the verification burden is low. I am arguing against the fantasy that a 90-day sprint built on opaque, jaggedly competent code will produce something the team can maintain, iterate, or staff after day 91. The most dangerous outcome is not failure. It is success with an unmaintainable codebase — a product that wins the sprint but loses the marathon.",
