te option expires silently. That expiration is invisible and irreversible. The Accelerationist is not pricing an option; they are issuing an unbounded, unpriced liability and calling it a hedge.\n\nThe Skeptic correctly diagnosed the power asymmetry: Product and Finance capture the upside of shipping velocity while Engineering and customers bear the downside of accumulated debt. But diagnosis is not prescription. The reason the Accelerationist's 40% threshold will not hold is structural, not behavioral. The team that sets the threshold — Engineering — lacks the institutional authority to halt deployment when Product leadership demands the ship date. This is the same regulatory capture dynamic the FAA faced before Congress gave it independent airworthiness directive authority in 1958. Boeing's 737 MAX crisis in 2018-2019 showed what happens when that independence erodes: internal safety signals existed but lacked the organizational power to override production schedules. A startup's engineering team facing a board-mandated 90-day deadline is in a structurally identical position. The fix is not a better metric. It is a designated safety reviewer with documented veto authority and no-blame protection — lightweight enough for a 10-person team, powerful enough to stop a catastrophic ship.\n\nThe correct governance mechanism is a real-time detect-then-mitigate pipeline operating at the module level, combined with differential adoption by risk tier. Concretely: accelerate LLM use for low-risk scaffolding like UI boilerplate and CRUD endpoints, while restricting it for authentication, payment processing, and data pipeline modules where a subtle defect can cascade to customers. This is not slowing down the sprint. It is steering velocity toward safe terrain. The detect-then-mitigate approach shifts quality assurance from retrospective sprint reviews to continuous, automated scanning of each LLM-generated module before merge — catching hallucinated API calls, phantom dependencies, and logic errors at the point of introduction rather than two sprints later. The overhead is bounded: static analysis tools like Semgrep or CodeQL already run in under 90 seconds per pull request. The alternative — discovering a hallucinated dependency in production at scale — costs orders of magnitude more.\n\nTwo assumptions underpin this argument, and policymakers should test them. First, LLM output non-determinism is irreducible enough that sprint-level defect averages are unreliable governance signals. If someone demonstrates that fixed model versions with locked temperature settings produce stable, reproducible defect profiles across architectures, this assumption weakens. Second, organizational power asymmetries will reliably override voluntary quality thresholds absent institutional independence. If a startup can show a track record of Engineering successfully halting a ship over Product objections using only a defect-rate trigger, this assumption weakens. Until both conditions are met, treating AI-generated code as a benign accelerant is building on sand. Progress is welcome — but only when the safety gates travel with the velocity, not behind it.",
