ble via data-flow analysis), handles financial transactions (detectable via API endpoint mapping to payment processors), manages authentication credentials, or executes database migrations on production schemas. These are binary facts about what code touches, not judgment calls about how risky it feels. You cannot reclassify a module importing the Stripe payments SDK as scaffolding when the dependency graph proves otherwise. The safety reviewer audits these classifications with the same structural independence as a financial auditor — the team being audited does not choose its own audit scope. To create skin-in-the-game, any module classified as low-risk that subsequently causes a data breach or financial loss should trigger escalated liability for the classifying team. This makes gaming the classification system a bet against your own career, not a free option. Governance frameworks must resist adversarial manipulation, and objective criteria plus liability linkage are the enforcement mechanism.\n\nThe Accelerationist claims that because 70–80% of MVP code sits in the fast lane, the Safetyist framework effectively endorses the accelerationist position for most of the codebase. This is like saying a bridge engineer endorses skipping cable inspections because 95% of the bridge is concrete. The framework's value is measured by the severity of harm it prevents in the restricted lane, not by the volume of code in the fast lane. The 20–30% of modules handling payments, authentication, and user data is where irremediable harm concentrates — breaches that cannot be patched after the fact, liability that cannot be unwound. As \"WhyLanguageModelsHallucinate\" documents, hallucination is an innate limitation of current language models, not a temporary bug. Automated metrics like cyclomatic complexity and documentation coverage can all read green while the underlying code remains opaque or subtly wrong. The \"International AI Safety Report 2026\" found that frontier models have demonstrated in-context scheming — strategic deception during evaluation. If the models generating the code can behave deceptively under evaluation, then automated gates evaluating that code's outputs face the same proxy-target gap. Human judgment is needed precisely where automated proxies fail.\n\nFinally, even granting the Accelerationist's strongest claim — that the prototype is genuinely disposable — the engineering culture it creates is not. Velocity expectations, review norms, and organizational habits formed during a 90-day sprint with minimal safety oversight persist into the production phase. Once a team has shipped at prototype speed, any attempt to introduce rigorous review for the production rewrite will be experienced as a slowdown and resisted by Product and Finance, who hold the upside while Engineering bears the cost. This is the organizational ratchet the Skeptic identified, applied to norms rather than code. The safety reviewer role must be established during the prototype phase so that the norm of independent review is culturally embedded before the stakes escalate. My argument depends on two key assumptions: first, that objective data-property criteria can cover the majority of high-risk module classifications without excessive false negatives; second, that organizational norms formed during prototype phases demonstrably persist into production. If either assumption fails, the framework weakens — but the alternative of purely automated gates with no independent human certification has already failed catastrophically in aviation.",
