nted failure modes in software engineering. It fails not because founders intend to keep the prototype, but because stakeholders demand features on the existing system rather than waiting for a rewrite, and the rewrite keeps getting deferred because it competes with revenue-generating feature work. AI-generated code makes this worse: the rewrite cost is higher because the code is opaque despite passing metric gates, while the pressure to keep shipping is stronger because velocity expectations were set by AI-assisted generation speed. My argument depends on two assumptions: first, that organizational incentives to defer rewrites are stronger than founder intentions to execute them; second, that proxy metrics for comprehensibility systematically diverge from actual comprehensibility under production pressure. If either assumption is wrong, the Accelerationist's framework holds.\n\nThe co-option of the Safetyist's risk-tiered framework creates a new governance gap that neither side has addressed. The Accelerationist claims 70-80% of MVP code is low-risk scaffolding belonging in the \"go fast\" tier. But who classifies modules into tiers? This creates the same gamification incentive documented in compute threshold debates: teams will reclassify modules as \"scaffolding\" to keep them in the fast tier, just as AI developers manipulate model architecture to stay below regulatory compute thresholds in the EU AI Act. Without independent verification of tier assignment, the team that benefits from speed is the same team that decides what counts as low-risk. The tiering framework is only as good as the independence and auditability of the classification process. The Accelerationist has proposed an enforcement mechanism for code quality but no enforcement mechanism for the enforcement mechanism. That is the meta-governance gap, and it is where the organizational discipline problem actually lives.",
